{
  "schema_version": "3.0",
  "key": "s_verbs",
  "concept_slug": "s_verbs",
  "grounding_mode": "fallback_no_wiki",
  "title": "Lexical s-verbs: synes, trives, finnes, møtes and ses",
  "cefr_level": "B1",
  "goal": "Recognize and use common s-verbs whose s is part of the verb meaning, not a passive ending.",
  "elements": [
    {
      "kind": "section",
      "role": "orient",
      "title": "When the s belongs to the verb",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Some Norwegian verbs end in "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "s",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " because that ending is part of the verb's own meaning. These are lexical s-verbs: verbs you learn as whole vocabulary items. Do not automatically read every verb ending in "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "s",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " as passive."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "rule",
          "statement": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "A lexical s-verb is learned with the "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "s",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "; the "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "s",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " is not a passive marker in that sentence."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Jeg synes filmen er interessant."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "I think the film is interesting."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Hun trives godt i Bergen."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "She is happy and doing well in Bergen."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "no": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Det finnes mange gode løsninger."
            }
          ],
          "en": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "There are many good solutions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "The passive is different: it says that something is done to something or someone. In "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "Boka brukes på kurset",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": ", the book is being used. In "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "Vi ses i morgen",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": ", nobody is being seen by an unnamed agent; it means we will see each other tomorrow."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "section",
      "role": "model",
      "title": "Core meanings to learn as whole verbs",
      "blocks": [
